### 1. Executive Summary

This report provides a detailed overview of the current IT systems, networks, servers, cloud infrastructure, and cybersecurity measures at TechWave Solutions Inc. It outlines the dependencies between various components and personnel, discusses key partnerships, and offers recommendations for future improvements to enhance operational efficiency and security posture.

---

### 2. Current IT Infrastructure Overview

#### 2.1. IT Systems

TechWave Solutions operates multiple enterprise resource planning (ERP) systems, including SAP S/4HANA, which integrates our financial, supply chain, and human resource management. The ERP system is managed by the IT Operations Manager, Sarah Thompson, who oversees a team of six IT specialists.

In addition, we utilize Customer Relationship Management (CRM) software, primarily Salesforce, to manage client interactions. The CRM is administered by the Sales Operations team led by Mark Sullivan.

#### 2.2. Network Architecture

The network architecture at TechWave is built on a robust framework utilizing Cisco Meraki switches and routers. The network is segmented into various Virtual Local Area Networks (VLANs) to ensure optimal performance and security. The network infrastructure is monitored by the Network Administrator, Jamie Lee, who uses SolarWinds for real-time monitoring and performance analytics.

The headquarters in San Francisco is connected to two regional offices in Austin, TX, and New York, NY, via dedicated MPLS lines, ensuring secure and efficient communications between locations.

#### 2.3. Server Environment

Our server environment consists of a mix of on-premises and cloud-based solutions. We maintain a data center in the San Francisco headquarters with 50 physical servers, including Dell PowerEdge R740 and HP ProLiant DL380 Gen10 servers, which support our enterprise applications and databases.

In addition, we leverage Amazon Web Services (AWS) for cloud services, primarily utilizing EC2 instances for application hosting and S3 for data storage. The cloud environment is managed by our Cloud Architect, Linda Chen, who is responsible for maintaining uptime and optimizing costs.

#### 2.4. Cloud Infrastructure

The cloud infrastructure is designed for high availability with a multi-AZ (Availability Zone) approach in AWS. We utilize AWS Lambda for serverless computing and Amazon RDS for managed database services. The cloud environment supports scalability for our growing workload demands, which have increased by 30% over the last year.

Our cloud security is reinforced by the use of AWS IAM (Identity and Access Management) policies, which ensure that access is granted based on the principle of least privilege.

